ith `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n \ndiff --git a/internal/config/defaults.go b/internal/config/defaults.go\nindex d99028e..c01e6c2 100644\n--- a/internal/config/defaults.go\n+++ b/internal/config/defaults.go\n@@ -174,6 +174,9 @@ func applyGlobals(res *Resolved, raw rawConfig, cfgPath string) Errors {\n \tif raw.gitleaksEnabled != nil {\n \t\tres.Redact.Gitleaks.Enabled = *raw.gitleaksEnabled\n